{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1337512-voice-of-the-mimic-copying-or-impersonating","id":1337512,"title":"Voice of the Mimic: Copying or Impersonating?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-09-23T11:07:50-07:00","id":1337512,"title":"Voice of the Mimic: Copying or Impersonating?","content":"Hello all,<br /> <br /> I have just returned to Storytelling Werewolf after a LONG hiatus, and I picked up W20 and hit the ground running.<br /> <br /> While getting set up for our new Chronicle, one of my players and I got in a discussion about the gift Voice of the Mimic - we were discussing if we thought the gift allowed its user to &quot;playback a recording&quot; or &quot;steal the voice of someone to perfectly impersonate them.&quot;<br /> <br /> Essentially, it came down to a difference in opinion about what &quot;mimicry&quot; means, and how words like imitate, mimic, impersonate, emulate, etc. all use each other to define themselves.<br /> <br /> The gift reads thusly: <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:-webkit-standard\"> <span style=\"color:#231F20\"><span style=\"font-family:Goudy\">Voice of the Mimic (Level One) </span></span><span style=\"color:#231F20\"><span style=\"font-family:Goudy\">\u2014 The Corax </span></span><span style=\"color:#231F20\"><span style=\"font-family:Goudy\">may perfectly mimic any voice or sound she has ever heard. A mynah-spirit teaches this Gift.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#231F20\"><span style=\"font-family:Goudy\">System: </span></span><span style=\"color:#231F20\"><span style=\"font-family:Goudy\">This Gift\u2019s effects are permanent.</span></span><br /> <br /> <br /> </span></span><br /> It is the inclusion of &quot;voice&quot; with sound, I think, that is causing this confusion. If the gift is just allowing a magical &quot;playback&quot; of something heard, then specifying the two is unnecessary, as someone speaking is already &quot;a sound,&quot; but because the gift does seem to be pointing to recreating a discrete event it does not seem like being able to copy a voice for original content is what is intended.<br /> <br /> We went back and forth, sometimes playfully, sometimes heatedly, about what &quot;mimic&quot; means, and what the words that are used to define mimcry mean...<br /> ...we looked at older versions of the gift for context clues - and saw that older editions provided rules to support the &quot;recorded playback&quot; notion, but flavor text to support the 'perfectly impersonate the voice&quot; interpretation; as &quot;voices and accents are covered by the scope of this gift.&quot;<br /> <br /> Okay, so Pumonca can also get this gift... let's look at their old gift, as it was updated to just accessing this one... and we are back to &quot;what does imitate&quot; mean?<br /> <br /> So I guess what I am asking here is does anyone (devs please weigh in if you see this) have any insight on what *was intended with this gift*?<br /> <br /> I am aware that as the ST, my interpretation and ruling is how it works in my game, and I have some pretty strong opinions about what the *actual* language of the gift means... but I also want to be respectful of my player's thoughts and opinion, so I figured I would ask the community (and the devs) what the gift is supposed to be capable of.<br /> <br /> For clarity, I believe that it allows perfect playback of a sound (including speech) that the character has heard - ie. This gift could be played like a recording of someone talking, or allow the character to connect to a fax machine to reprint the information they overheard playing from a fax machine, etc. I do NOT believe that by hearing a security guard say &quot;Yeah, I looked, there was nothing there,&quot; a character could then pick up a radio and say &quot;Hey Jonny, come check this out&quot; in that guard's voice. In my interpretation, original content in a mimicked voice would require the &quot;audio editing&quot; of repeating each word from an existing memory, stringing them together into a unique sentence, but bearing the original tones/inflections/etc. of the original sound (kind of like you see in movies where they stitch together several recordings of someone to fool voice activation, etc.).<br /> <br /> My player believes that the gift does allow the playback function I describe above, but that because the gift covers &quot;voice and accent&quot; in scope, it also would allow original content in someone's voice because the gift has allowed you to mimic their &quot;voice&quot; not &quot;what they just said.&quot;<br /> <br /> So, any thoughts/ideas (or hopefully dev input)?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25675-anna-fianna","name":"Anna Fianna","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25675_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-09-23T18:39:10-07:00","id":1337566,"title":"","content":"How useful is the Gift if its only use is to perfectly playback a voice or sound the person heard? I would wager the Gift might never get used at all during a chronicle, or perhaps only a few times. Other then a very contrived scenario, it would be useless. I don't believe Gifts are meant to be useless.<br /> <br /> So in terms of voices at least, I would say the person could imitate the voice - at least within reason. A normal speaking voice, but not the person's singing voice, or not how the person sounds when yelling. Nor would it allow the character to speak words only known by or correctly pronounced by the other person, but not by them. So there is always a chance that the mimicry could be found out if the character was too blatant about it.<br /> <br /> In general for questions like this, one of my guidelines is that to make sure the power is useful, but not exploited.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-09-24T03:59:49-07:00","id":1337613,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1337512-voice-of-the-mimic-copying-or-impersonating?p=1337566#post1337566\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How useful is the Gift if its only use is to perfectly playback a voice or sound the person heard? I would wager the Gift might never get used at all during a chronicle, or perhaps only a few times. Other then a very contrived scenario, it would be useless. I don't believe Gifts are meant to be useless.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, there are gifts to perfectly recall memories, including what one heard. But such are the domain of the Galliard (or Mokol\u00e9 maybe).<br /> <br /> My guess is, that it allows to speak in a manner that when heard even by someone very familiar with said voice is reasonably mistaken as the voice of that person. Likewise, one could mimic the sound of a 33k modem or a pneumatic hammer with absolute accuracy, as in that the sound is a perfect match.<br /> <br /> As for content, I'd say, the gift doesn't allow for that (unless other gifts or merits come into play). Meaning, the gift user would only speak English, using the gift on Pasquale's voice, only heard in her French native tongue, would only allow to speak English in a dialect that would fit Pasquale's. If the gift user spoke French, using Pasquale's voice would be dialect free.<br /> Same goes for them 33k modem. Sure, it sounds like said modem, but without understanding the sounds, the corax couldn't make a proper connection establishing handshake with another modem by simply calling it on the telephone - for the corax probably lacks the understanding of what these sounds mean.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-09-24T06:42:11-07:00","id":1337625,"title":"","content":"I spent a long time responding to this, and lost it do to an accidental page refresh... /sigh. Thank you both for responding. To keep from grinding my teeth recreating several paragraphs I have already written, I just pulled quotes and will respond to them directly - to preface, this is meant as discussion/brainstorming/reasoning-it-out, and none is intended as combative in tone. Also, yes, I get that this is a LOT of chewing on a simple question - part of it is wanting to make the right call, and some of it is just the fun of the debate of trying to figure it out.<br /> <br /> So here we go...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How useful is the Gift if its only use is to perfectly playback a voice or sound the person heard? I would wager the Gift might never get used at all during a chronicle, or perhaps only a few times. Other then a very contrived scenario, it would be useless. I don't believe Gifts are meant to be useless. </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In general for questions like this, one of my guidelines is that to make sure the power is useful, but not exploited. </div> </div> </div>To me, the ability to perfectly recreate something heard is *extremely* useful, and a creative player could use that every single session - a less creative player could probably still find use for it multiple times in a given story, let alone a whole chronicle. Using sounds to mock and terrify prey (such as out the Yautja/Predators use it in their movies), copying sounds to confound security systems or guards (voice activated devices, misleading radio calls), manipulating members of the flock (making a server or clerk go try to answer a phone you heard ring earlier, or make them think they are being called from another room *again*). Also, how much more trustworthy is a message if it is heard in the original speaker's voice? It is a Corax gift, after all...<br /> <br /> But here is the kicker - it is a Level One gift. I am going to stray a bit into some game-balance musings/philosophy here, so please bear with me.<br /> <br /> With a handful of exceptions, most Level One gifts achieve things that could be done with moderate effort or exertion, or replace the need for reasonable preparation or equipment. Aside from a debate about whether or not gifts should be seen as &quot;spiritual tricks one has picked up&quot; or as magic spells or as super powers, other Level One gifts generally prevent you from needing to carry lockpicks, or a gallon of water, or take the time to cover your tracks. Sure, there are obvious exceptions - there is no real equivalent for Mother's Touch - but even Sense Wyrm and Mindspeak, as supernatural as they are, really just approximate and improve upon the idea of carrying a device like a geiger-counter or wearing headsets. Even gifts that used to have much more fantastic effects, such as Blur of the Milky Eye and Scent of Running Water, have been dialed back to be modifiers to skills you can fail instead of being straight up invisibility to the various senses.<br /> <br /> Are there Level One gifts that circumvent mastery of a skill? Sure. And Mimicry is a skill, right? With enough practice, a normal human can imitate someone with passable, some times eerie, accuracy. City Running, for instance, lets all Garou be masters of Parkour. Surely Voice of the Mimic can allow you to be a master of mimicry. Here is my struggle with that: City Running is temporary, and requires a *cost* - it also doesn't eliminate *all* skill rolls (the ones still necessary do get a reduced difficulty, however). Voice of the Mimic is *always* on, and requires *nothing* to activate - no temporary points, not even a reflexive skill roll that you could fail.<br /> <br /> So, all that said, I *absolutely* agree with you that all a player's gifts should be useful. Perhaps I just have a different view on how useful being a living recording-machine is? Also, mechanically speaking, always being able to use a mastery-level skill capability with no chance of failure, with nearly no (or at least very little) pre-work necessary - at no mechanical cost to the character - seems outside the scope of a Level One gift.<br /> <br /> I guess that is what it comes down to, for me, really - it is not a question of &quot;not useful or useful,&quot; it is a question of &quot;useful or broken.&quot; There are a lot of times when it would be useful to be able to replay sounds, but the ability to perfectly recreate anyone's voice while saying whatever you want, with no chance of failure or magical price to be paid, just because one time you heard them say &quot;hello?&quot; Oof.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So in terms of voices at least, I would say the person could imitate the voice - at least within reason. A normal speaking voice, but not the person's singing voice, or not how the person sounds when yelling. Nor would it allow the character to speak words only known by or correctly pronounced by the other person, but not by them. So there is always a chance that the mimicry could be found out if the character was too blatant about it. </div> </div> </div>In lock step with you here. If the gift *can* allow you to impersonate them while saying whatever you want, I would absolutely agree that you wouldn't know how their voice changes during a yell, or even a whisper (or scream, or moan, etc.) without hearing that as well. You would only have access to the kind of voice they were using at the time of witness. Same thing would apply if you heard someone scream, but not speak - you have no idea what their speaking voice sounds like.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well, there are gifts to perfectly recall memories, including what one heard. But such are the domain of the Galliard (or Mokol\u00e9 maybe). </div> </div> </div>...or a super cheap Merit (Eidetic Memory). Further, one could even argue that for its own purposes, specifically, you would not even *need* Eidetic Memory or a gift to recall something. The gift itself states that to reproduce a sound, you only have to have heard it - you don't even have to struggle to remember it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> My guess is, that it allows to speak in a manner that when heard even by someone very familiar with said voice is reasonably mistaken as the voice of that person. Likewise, one could mimic the sound of a 33k modem or a pneumatic hammer with absolute accuracy, as in that the sound is a perfect match. </div> </div> </div>\u200b<br /> <br /> This is a devil's advocate question, not me being snarky: If you can perfectly recreate the sound of a power tool or the complex hiss of a modem, why would a voice (regardless of content) only be reasonably mistaken for the original? Should the gift not either always allow perfect recreation or always be limited to reasonable approximation?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As for content, I'd say, the gift doesn't allow for that (unless other gifts or merits come into play). Meaning, the gift user would only speak English, using the gift on Pasquale's voice, only heard in her French native tongue, would only allow to speak English in a dialect that would fit Pasquale's. If the gift user spoke French, using Pasquale's voice would be dialect free.<br /> Same goes for them 33k modem. Sure, it sounds like said modem, but without understanding the sounds, the corax couldn't make a proper connection establishing handshake with another modem by simply calling it on the telephone - for the corax probably lacks the understanding of what these sounds mean. </div> </div> </div>I will be honest, this one loses me a bit - and maybe I am not reading it correctly, or am getting hung up on dialect vs. accent. Okay, so if a Garou who speaks only English, not English and French, hears someone speaking in French, what would the gift do? Well, if it is the &quot;recording&quot; interpretation, the answer is easy - the Garou can playback what they heard (which speaks *nothing* about whether or not they have any idea what they are saying). If we are using the &quot;impersonation&quot; interpretation, I would argue that they know how to emulate the person's voice - meaning it would sound like the French person speaking in English (as if the French person was perfectly emulating the Garou's native accent). Accent-ception! I sound like you sounding like you sound like me! Now, if we are going to use the *old* wording of the gift to interpret the current gift, &quot;accent&quot; is within the scope of the gift, so I would argue that this English-speaking Garou could speak in the French-speaker's voice, in English, with the French-speaker's accurate French accent. Once again, I am you, as if you are speaking my language.<br /> <br /> When it comes to the modem thing, nothing about the gift grants any understanding of the noise - you are correct there - so hearing a gunshot doesn't tell the Corax the calibre, nor does recreating the modem sound tell the Corax what any of it means. The sound is just being played, and if that sound is enough to create an effect then yay! If the modem on the other end asks a question the Corax can't answer, then boo!<br /> <br /> But I think the modem example perfectly speaks to my issue with using this gift to *impersonate* rather than *play back* someone's voice. As anyone who has tried to do an accent, or to reproduce someone's voice, can tell you - it takes listening and practicing to reproduce a single word or phrase as someone else, with any consistency. I don't just mean for comedic effect - you can lazily reproduce someone's voice enough for a joke in a couple tries; but could you fool their mom on the phone? And specific to accents - knowing how to say one phrase sometimes doesn't prepare you at all for saying another phrase in that accent, if the sentence structure has profoundly changed, or you are using new vowel sounds, etc. Knowing how someone with a different accent says &quot;hello&quot; does not teach you how they might say &quot;thunderstorm.&quot; The sounds are just so different that you cannot necessarily approximate them accurately without more of a frame of reference. So, I wonder, where does one draw the line between the gift allowing you to copy and the gift allowing you to generate data you don't have?<br /> <br /> Yes the gift is magic, yes this is fiction, I get all that, but from a standpoint of rationality - if a Corax can carry on a perfectly convincing conversation with your grandma, because they heard you say &quot;Ow! I just stubbed my toe on that!&quot; in a grocery store parking lot, one time last week... why can he not have a complete conversation with another modem because he heard a modem one time? Sure, maybe we argue that he would need to know binary or C++ (or whatever the language is that modem noise is called), but shouldn't he be able to buy the 1-pt Language Merit &quot;Modemspeak&quot; and use Voice of the Mimic to be able to generate unique modem noises?<br /> <br /> It just starts to feel like the gift is WAY too abusable if you start with the assumption that it is not just a recording/playback.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25675,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25675-anna-fianna","user":"Anna Fianna","date":".  Duplicate word elimination. 09-24-2019, 09:47 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25675-anna-fianna","name":"Anna Fianna","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25675_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-09-24T13:04:57-07:00","id":1337678,"title":"","content":"When I ran a game with a Corax one of the players took the gift, and I just assumed it allowed them to copy someone's voice. Not simply their words, but their voice. I figured if it was supposed to just be a magical recording device that could only capture the words someone spoke it would have said, &quot;words&quot; but by using &quot;voice&quot; that implies to me that you can use your own words, it's just that they will come out in the voice of the person you are mimicking.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-09-24T16:44:07-07:00","id":1337729,"title":"","content":"I'd personally run it like a recording, the way parrots and lyre birds can say &quot;polly want a cracker&quot; or *chainsaw*, but can't improvise to say &quot;Polly want to hack a computer&quot; or *chainsaw through metal*.<br /> <br /> Run it however you find fun, really.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.dropbox.com/s/ofii86boiyk38ng/DtDRandomForms.zip\" target=\"_blank\">Open the gates of Hell: Random Demon Generator.</a><br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1X6qQzoAPrSRNzl3cQcMWasoPv3eK56sIlAOTeay61No/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Gather an army of Rage! Random Garou Generator.</a><br /> Shapeshifting Historian<br /> Writer - Werewolf: the Apocalypse, Werewolf: the Forsaken, Exalted 3e, TC: \u00c6on","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/167-nikink","name":"nikink","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"360"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-09-25T10:21:50-07:00","id":1337823,"title":"","content":"Personally I'd keep it simple:<br /> <br /> <i>&quot;Voice of the mimic lets you make any sound you remember or actually can imagine</i>&quot;.<br /> Remember how a chainsaw sounds? You can make the sound.<br /> Heard a person and can imagine them saying things they have not actually said, in their voice? (Just like you are now reading the following text in the voice of your favorite actor). You can make that sound. In the case of the latter, it *might* be enough to fool very close associates of them over the phone - it's hard to tell since we can't quite experiment with that yet reliably <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-09-25T16:18:31-07:00","id":1337868,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1337512-voice-of-the-mimic-copying-or-impersonating?p=1337678#post1337678\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When I ran a game with a Corax one of the players took the gift, and I just assumed it allowed them to copy someone's voice. Not simply their words, but their voice. I figured if it was supposed to just be a magical recording device that could only capture the words someone spoke it would have said, &quot;words&quot; but by using &quot;voice&quot; that implies to me that you can use your own words, it's just that they will come out in the voice of the person you are mimicking.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair - I had just read the use of &quot;voice&quot; as being specified so that undesirables wouldn't feel compelled to argue &quot;Um, no, the letter 'a' is a *sound*, you can't copy him because his words are a string of several sounds, not *a* sound.&quot; Essentially a way of making sure you know &quot;Hey Joe, look over there!&quot; was &quot;a sound,&quot; for rules-as-intended purposes. Not sure if that makes sense to anyone but me, but that is how I had always read the reason they said sound *and* voice... until my player recently asked this question.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25675,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25675-anna-fianna","user":"Anna Fianna","date":".  Fixing a typo... 09-25-2019, 07:19 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25675-anna-fianna","name":"Anna Fianna","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25675_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-09-26T07:55:29-07:00","id":1337983,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Anna Fianna</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1337512-voice-of-the-mimic-copying-or-impersonating?p=1337625#post1337625\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is a devil's advocate question, not me being snarky: If you can perfectly recreate the sound of a power tool or the complex hiss of a modem, why would a voice (regardless of content) only be reasonably mistaken for the original? Should the gift not either always allow perfect recreation or always be limited to reasonable approximation?</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd argue for the same reason, you can't re-create an accurate modem handshake: Content. Being perfect in regards to physical characteristics of the voice, like pitch and such, would make peop\u00f6\u00f6e reasonably mistake you for the owner of that voice, if the said words don't make the listener question the authenticity.<br /> If I hear my best friend's voice order shellfish, despite being highly allergic, might make me question the situation. <br /> <br /> But, you are right my statement of the accent in the voice wasn't well thought through. I wanted to point out, that the user doesn't get the knowledge of a foreign language. Also, the giftuser should speak in his native tongue without accent, even if the original owner of the voice would.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}